# =====================================================================================
# CREDENTIALS - NEVER COMMIT THESE.
#
# .env holds live SQL Server logins for five databases and the NVIDIA NIM API key.
# Once a secret is committed it stays in the repository's HISTORY forever, even after
# the file is deleted - so this file has to exist BEFORE the first push, not after.
#
# Moving to a second machine, .env is copied across BY HAND, once, outside GitHub.
# =====================================================================================
.env
.env.*
.env.bak
!.env.example

# 🔴 THE DOTLESS COPIES. Found on the office desktop 2026-09-16 by the Claude session there:
# a file named `env` - no dot - sitting in the clone with live SQL logins for five databases and
# the Anthropic key in it, matching NONE of the four patterns above. `git check-ignore` returned
# exit 1: NOT ignored. It was the only untracked file in the repo, so a single `git add -A` would
# have put live credentials into permanent history - the exact outcome the block at the top of this
# file exists to prevent, defeated by a missing full stop.
#
# It gets there honestly: Teams and Windows both dislike a leading dot, so `.env` becomes `env` or
# `env.txt` in transit, and the person who renames it to send it is not the person who remembers to
# rename it back. The ignore list has to cover the shapes the file arrives in, not just the shape
# it is supposed to have.
env
env.txt
env.bak
*.env

# =====================================================================================
# NOT IGNORED, DELIBERATELY - the irreplaceable files live under output/ and they must
# travel between machines. Do NOT add output/ to this list:
#
#   output/Taxonomy/*DECISIONS NEEDED*.xlsx   583 of Sameer's rulings. It is the only
#                                             output file read back as an INPUT, so the
#                                             rulings exist NOWHERE ELSE.
#   output/Checked/*.xlsx                     completed analyst workbooks - their
#                                             answers exist nowhere else until ingested.
#
# The whole project is ~18 MB. There is no size argument for leaving them out, and
# losing a ruling because it sat on the other machine is a real and expensive failure.
# =====================================================================================

# Run-machine state under output/logs/ — a NARROW exception to the rule above, and narrow on
# purpose. Everything else under output/ stays tracked because it is irreplaceable; these two are
# neither irreplaceable nor safe to move between machines.
#
#   supervise.lock   The PID of the supervisor ON THIS MACHINE. It is actively HARMFUL if it
#                    travels: a lock naming a PID that means something else on the desktop is
#                    exactly how the "is another supervisor running?" check gets a wrong answer.
#   judge-*.log      Verbatim judge output, one file per restart attempt. A single full production
#                    run writes ~278,600 batch lines; these reach tens of MB and are regenerable.
#
# ⚠️ supervise-*.log is DELIBERATELY NOT IGNORED. It is small, and it is the RESTART RECORD — the
# only measurement of how often the judge actually trips (RUN_LOG Finding 133 rests on n=1). Losing
# it would put us back to guessing.
output/logs/supervise.lock
output/logs/judge-*.log

# Virtual environments. A venv created INSIDE the clone is thousands of untracked files sitting
# next to the code; `git add -A` would commit an entire Python installation. The office desktop has
# one (2026-09-16), so this is not hypothetical.
.venv/
venv/
ENV/

# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]

# Excel lock files - created while a workbook is open, meaningless to anyone else
~$*.xlsx
~$*.csv

# OS noise
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
.DS_Store
